[SPEAKER_00]: Like, that's why it's a good friend.

166
00:18:42,456 --> 00:18:43,817
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because of how they make you feel.

167
00:18:44,257 --> 00:18:45,658
[SPEAKER_00]: In fact, even the Buddha said,

168
00:18:48,117 --> 00:18:56,122
[SPEAKER_00]: When a Brahmin went to ask him, is it that you always... that you would never say anything that would displease someone?

169
00:18:56,803 --> 00:19:00,565
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you claim to be, you know, the foremost compassionate being or something.

170
00:19:00,665 --> 00:19:01,346
[SPEAKER_00]: And then he said, no.

171
00:19:01,966 --> 00:19:07,670
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes, it's like, he gave a simile, it's like when a baby swallows something and it's about to choke.

172
00:19:08,410 --> 00:19:21,537
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you know, a normal person, sensible person, would even, like, put their hands in their mouth and even take out blood if it's necessary to save their life.

173
00:19:21,677 --> 00:19:28,481
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said it's the same thing when he needs to, you know, point out the flaws in people's views and stuff, which is what

174
00:19:28,941 --> 00:19:30,183
[SPEAKER_00]: But he was doing most of the time.

175
00:19:30,263 --> 00:19:39,316
[SPEAKER_00]: And, you know, many people might not feel like that's a kalyāṇamitta, a good friend, because it's not really making you feel very good most of the time.

176
00:19:39,376 --> 00:19:40,378
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

177
00:19:40,818 --> 00:19:43,422
[SPEAKER_00]: And even to Ānanda, his own disciple,

178
00:19:44,743 --> 00:20:05,048
[SPEAKER_00]: His attendant, there was a case where he was... many monks, actually, were gathering together for the for the robe season festival, and they were gathering in these tents and stuff, and then the Buddha came and asked, like, why is it... why is it that there seem to be so many monks hanging around here?

179
00:20:05,908 --> 00:20:11,290
[SPEAKER_00]: And, I mean, it was supposedly for a good reason, for sewing these robes and stuff, but the Buddha said,

180
00:20:12,150 --> 00:20:19,715
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, he basically admonished all of them for not living in solitude, for delighting in company.

181
00:20:19,816 --> 00:20:28,021
[SPEAKER_00]: And to Ānanda, his own attendant, he said, like, I won't treat you like a potter would treat his valuable pots.

182
00:20:28,642 --> 00:20:34,526
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, I will smash you and then the core is what will remain.

183
00:20:34,946 --> 00:20:35,487
[SPEAKER_00]: The core that

184
00:20:36,307 --> 00:20:43,494
[SPEAKER_00]: A good friend is the friend who will stage an intervention for you, not the friend who's giving you free drugs.

185
00:20:43,754 --> 00:20:44,074
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

186
00:20:44,094 --> 00:20:44,615
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is not...

187
00:21:02,826 --> 00:21:05,848
[SPEAKER_04]: It's basically not who seems like a good friend.

188
00:21:05,868 --> 00:21:18,796
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, and this kind of non-violent communication that you point out, like, not hurting people's feelings, like, kind of imply the fact that, oh, people, other people can impact my feeling, and I can impact their feeling.

189
00:21:19,357 --> 00:21:21,258
[SPEAKER_03]: So, there is kind of this implication of...

190
00:21:22,304 --> 00:21:27,207
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm responsible for them, and you are responsible for me, and then we try to kind of flow together like that.

191
00:21:27,747 --> 00:21:36,651
[SPEAKER_00]: It's kind of a bit... Yeah, each person, like, that's already overlooking the responsibility that each person has for suffering in the first place.

192
00:21:37,532 --> 00:21:46,837
[SPEAKER_02]: Each person, it doesn't matter how much you insult someone, obviously it doesn't mean you should do it, but if a person suffers, that's because they haven't understood.

193
00:21:47,677 --> 00:21:48,418
[SPEAKER_00]: The way of suffering.

194
00:21:48,438 --> 00:22:08,754
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, by trying to create these safe spaces and trying to think that that's what creating a good community is about, like just avoid offending people at all costs, you are kind of implying that all suffering is out there in the things that other people do to you and not internally in your own views, fundamentally.

195
00:22:09,375 --> 00:22:10,135
[SPEAKER_00]: And another thing is

196
00:22:11,116 --> 00:22:27,202
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, it's important to point out that, as well, that it's not bad in itself to try to be gentle and be polite, because that's a... that leads to more fluent interaction between people, actually, in the mundane sense.

197
00:22:27,262 --> 00:22:28,642
[SPEAKER_00]: But the problem is not that.

198
00:22:28,662 --> 00:22:35,305
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is when you prioritize this, and then, because you automatically forget that there is something more important,

199
00:22:35,985 --> 00:22:37,746
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is, like, right view.

200
00:22:38,287 --> 00:22:46,912
[SPEAKER_00]: But the Buddha would be, as you would say, he would use gentle speech at the right time, and he would know the right time for using not so gentle speech.

201
00:22:47,673 --> 00:22:52,596
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he has a more fundamental criteria than just how am I making people feel.

202
00:22:53,216 --> 00:22:56,518
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why sometimes he would be calling monks inept man and stuff.

203
00:22:57,179 --> 00:23:01,521
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that was the right thing to do at that time.

204
00:23:01,642 --> 00:23:03,463
[SPEAKER_00]: But I think he did that only with monks, though.

205
00:23:04,423 --> 00:23:04,904
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, of course.

206
00:23:05,124 --> 00:23:13,671
[SPEAKER_00]: Because people who had already subscribed to the training and basically accepted him as their guidance.

207
00:23:13,711 --> 00:23:14,311
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's right.

208
00:23:14,631 --> 00:23:23,959
[SPEAKER_00]: So, well, actually, he also, when there were these other ascetics of other sects, we'll also use the same term,

209
00:23:24,559 --> 00:23:44,051
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, the point is, like, you wouldn't... if you prioritize just that superficial aspect, you will become complacent in thinking that on account of this, yeah, you are factually avoiding, like, some chunk of the amount of unwholesome stuff.

210
00:23:44,692 --> 00:23:47,874
[SPEAKER_00]: But you would be overlooking that there's way more work to be done

211
00:23:48,534 --> 00:23:50,515
[SPEAKER_00]: on the level of those background intentions.

212
00:23:50,535 --> 00:23:51,336
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's really the problem.

213
00:23:51,356 --> 00:23:52,216
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not that it's wrong.

214
00:23:52,276 --> 00:23:54,597
[SPEAKER_00]: It's that people stop there.

215
00:23:55,018 --> 00:23:55,118
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

216
00:23:55,278 --> 00:23:57,639
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, don't say that there's more work to be done.

217
00:23:57,679 --> 00:24:01,281
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, the further work is the meditation techniques and the focusing and stuff.

218
00:24:01,981 --> 00:24:03,862
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that I don't even then need to

219
00:24:05,043 --> 00:24:08,085
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't want to be questioning my intentions and look at that level.

220
00:24:08,505 --> 00:24:16,689
[SPEAKER_00]: I just leave it there, leave the house half-painted, and then go somewhere else during the meditation session.

221
00:24:17,430 --> 00:24:27,535
[SPEAKER_00]: And you're not really cultivating the stuff that would make you... the type of meditation that would make you discern those unwholesome intentions when they're there.

222
00:24:27,915 --> 00:24:31,496
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that was actually the Buddha's advice to his own son, Rahula.

223
00:24:31,656 --> 00:24:33,997
[SPEAKER_01]: There's this whole discourse, which deals with speech.

224
00:24:34,617 --> 00:24:37,698
[SPEAKER_01]: And he says, like, you know, before you... What were the three?

225
00:24:37,918 --> 00:24:38,918
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

226
00:24:39,098 --> 00:24:43,099
[SPEAKER_01]: So, before you break out into speech, consider, is what I'm about to do wholesome?

227
00:24:43,539 --> 00:24:47,520
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, while you are talking... And so, this is the sati.

228
00:24:47,760 --> 00:24:49,581
[SPEAKER_01]: This is the mindfulness for the recollection.

229
00:24:49,621 --> 00:24:53,542
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, while you are talking, keep your aim in mind, like, is this still wholesome?

230
00:24:53,562 --> 00:24:53,802
[SPEAKER_01]: Because

231
00:24:54,747 --> 00:25:07,110
[SPEAKER_01]: It's very easy to start talking, because, you see, you might start talking with a wholesome intention, but you just lose yourself in your own speech, and while you're talking, it sort of deviates, and you end up almost doing the exact opposite of what you want.

232
00:25:07,670 --> 00:25:19,652
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to be on the ball while speaking, and then afterwards, perhaps when you are on your own in seclusion, you can still look back and consider, was that speech now actually wholesome or not?

233
00:25:20,392 --> 00:25:23,693
[SPEAKER_02]: And see, the... that wouldn't be possible

234
00:25:24,393 --> 00:25:32,475
[SPEAKER_02]: That what you just said, that the intention changes from unwholesome to unwholesome, if it were in the content of what you're saying.

235
00:25:32,775 --> 00:25:37,976
[SPEAKER_00]: It's really the mind changes its inclination towards the situation.

236
00:25:38,916 --> 00:25:46,398
[SPEAKER_00]: Some form of greed that wasn't there arises, but you might be talking about the same topic, saying the same words that you might have otherwise said.

237
00:25:47,058 --> 00:25:53,720
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing really changed on that on that level, but you internally now have a different impulse, a different

238
00:25:54,720 --> 00:25:58,443
[SPEAKER_00]: There's something different that's pushing you now to say these same things.

239
00:25:59,004 --> 00:26:00,105
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what makes it wrong.

240
00:26:00,125 --> 00:26:06,430
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so... Yeah, you... it's always has to be recognized.

241
00:26:06,470 --> 00:26:09,172
[SPEAKER_00]: The... as the Buddha said, action is intention.

242
00:26:09,612 --> 00:26:09,832
[SPEAKER_00]: Hmm.

243
00:26:10,233 --> 00:26:11,334
[SPEAKER_00]: So anything unwholesome...

244
00:26:12,455 --> 00:26:13,256
[SPEAKER_02]: cetanā is the word.

245
00:26:13,296 --> 00:26:23,408
[SPEAKER_02]: Anything unwholesome has to be that, not just like, you know, he didn't say action is the the movements you make with your hands and the sounds that come from your mouth.

246
00:26:23,608 --> 00:26:23,808
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

247
00:26:24,069 --> 00:26:24,850
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the intention.

248
00:26:24,910 --> 00:26:30,697
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you purify that internal level by recognizing it first and foremost,

249
00:26:31,377 --> 00:26:34,399
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you wouldn't have to worry about what you say externally.

250
00:26:35,020 --> 00:26:42,585
[SPEAKER_00]: You purify the source of the river in order for the, you know, later current to be clean.

251
00:26:42,745 --> 00:26:45,447
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't, like, start from the ground up.

252
00:26:45,567 --> 00:26:49,070
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just wasted, wasting effort and you're never really going to get anywhere.

253
00:26:49,090 --> 00:26:50,311
[SPEAKER_00]: So, eh...

254
00:26:51,311 --> 00:26:53,193
[SPEAKER_00]: That's essentially what's happening most of the time.

255
00:26:53,213 --> 00:27:01,618
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you would be... by trying to control your expressions and stuff, and thinking, okay, this is it.

256
00:27:01,638 --> 00:27:03,720
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't look... you don't look any further.

257
00:27:04,060 --> 00:27:16,168
[SPEAKER_02]: And... that's another thing with... the... like, when people have, for example, restless mental states, and worry, and a lot of...

258
00:27:17,068 --> 00:27:27,316
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, hectic thoughts and stuff, they would think, well, I need to now use a concentration technique to calm myself down.

259
00:27:27,956 --> 00:27:36,683
[SPEAKER_00]: But really, if you were actually restraining your speech rightly, first of all your actions, and then your speech,

260
00:27:37,776 --> 00:27:41,978
[SPEAKER_00]: You would see... you refrain from all this useless talk.

261
00:27:42,498 --> 00:27:47,160
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha defined the ten topics that are not worthy of discussion.

262
00:27:47,620 --> 00:27:57,044
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, talk about politics, food, sensuality, kings, clothes, money, relatives.

263
00:27:58,865 --> 00:28:04,950
[SPEAKER_00]: gossip, it's called like talk of the well, like when people would go to a well and they would get it.

264
00:28:04,970 --> 00:28:06,791
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, street talk and talk of the well.

265
00:28:06,972 --> 00:28:13,236
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you refrain from that stuff, then you won't have in the first place these

266
00:28:14,757 --> 00:28:31,550
[SPEAKER_01]: So, rather than trying to solve the problem afterwards, when the mind is already spinning out of control, you are saying that you find yourself in such a state,

267
00:28:32,837 --> 00:28:36,379
[SPEAKER_01]: One pillar there is the unrestrained speech on the past.

268
00:28:36,479 --> 00:28:36,639
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

269
00:28:36,679 --> 00:28:40,401
[SPEAKER_02]: So, instead of trying to clean something, then don't get it dirty in the first place.

270
00:28:40,761 --> 00:28:40,941
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

271
00:28:41,081 --> 00:28:43,222
[SPEAKER_02]: Instead of needing to clean after the fact.

272
00:28:43,262 --> 00:28:48,025
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you would see that then the purity

273
00:28:49,455 --> 00:28:55,200
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, it would become something that you no longer had to be hoping for or anticipating.

274
00:28:55,381 --> 00:28:58,944
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you know, how people would say, well, today my meditation was not good.

275
00:28:59,825 --> 00:29:02,727
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's almost like, well, that's that's that's just something random.

276
00:29:02,968 --> 00:29:05,230
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it was a bad day.

277
00:29:05,310 --> 00:29:05,730
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

278
00:29:05,770 --> 00:29:07,071
[SPEAKER_00]: But no, absolutely not.

279
00:29:07,091 --> 00:29:10,294
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, purity is entirely in your hands.

280
00:29:10,575 --> 00:29:14,698
[SPEAKER_00]: If you didn't do anything unwholesome, you can't be impure, even if you want to.

