00:29:14,919 --> 00:29:15,079
[SPEAKER_00]: Like,

282
00:29:16,300 --> 00:29:27,267
[SPEAKER_00]: If during the whole day you didn't engage in any unwholesome intentions, and right now you're not engaging in any unwholesome intentions, it's impossible to have a bad day in terms of your dhamma practice.

283
00:29:27,507 --> 00:29:27,707
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

284
00:29:27,968 --> 00:29:32,531
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it doesn't mean it will be pleasant, but again... That's not the criteria.

285
00:29:32,551 --> 00:29:33,672
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not the criterion.

286
00:29:33,692 --> 00:29:37,835
[SPEAKER_00]: The criterion is, am I giving in to aversion when there is displeasure?

287
00:29:38,956 --> 00:29:46,373
[SPEAKER_02]: And that would give rise to a different kind of peace than the peace that comes when sometimes your meditation technique works.

288
00:29:46,533 --> 00:29:47,655
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a different...

289
00:29:53,206 --> 00:30:03,675
[SPEAKER_01]: Earlier, you played devil's advocate for minutes and brought up the, let's say, friendly and amiable talk that the Buddha sometimes has, you know, when people come to see him.

290
00:30:03,975 --> 00:30:12,203
[SPEAKER_01]: And you should distinguish that from the gossip or the, let's say, idle talk, which is clearly unwholesome.

291
00:30:13,087 --> 00:30:18,151
[SPEAKER_01]: And it occurs to me that gossip in particular is a good example to reveal inauthenticity.

292
00:30:18,751 --> 00:30:27,999
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, inauthenticity can be described as just uncritically taking over the views and the opinions and... or the opinions and the beliefs, rather.

293
00:30:28,279 --> 00:30:38,447
[SPEAKER_01]: So, without questioning whether they are true, you take them over and then you assume that they are just disconnected ideas latent in the back of your mind and that they do not affect how you act by body and by speech.

294
00:30:39,408 --> 00:30:40,729
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not like that.

295
00:30:40,969 --> 00:30:49,519
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, furthermore, having those ideas already and seeing them as fairly harmless and innocent, you then pass them on to others.

296
00:30:50,400 --> 00:30:52,943
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, that's what gossip does.

297
00:30:52,983 --> 00:30:55,686
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it has that similar structure.

298
00:30:55,786 --> 00:30:56,567
[SPEAKER_01]: You hear something,

299
00:30:57,308 --> 00:30:59,409
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't even can be bothered whether it's correct or not.

300
00:30:59,710 --> 00:31:03,372
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't see that you have a responsibility for what you accept and what you reject.

301
00:31:03,712 --> 00:31:08,715
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, on top of that, you pass it on to others, which is also within the sphere of your responsibility.

302
00:31:09,296 --> 00:31:12,178
[SPEAKER_01]: So, to to fully reframe

303
00:31:12,918 --> 00:31:24,089
[SPEAKER_01]: From just passing something on without being reasonably clear whether it's correct or not, that's already on the level of speech moving in in the direction of authenticity.

304
00:31:24,549 --> 00:31:33,057
[SPEAKER_01]: But even then, if you are more or less clear whether something is correct, it still doesn't necessarily mean that you should therefore pass it on to others.

305
00:31:33,437 --> 00:31:37,821
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whether you then break out into speech, so to speak,

306
00:31:38,642 --> 00:31:42,846
