314
00:32:48,403 --> 00:33:00,510
[SPEAKER_04]: I was just going to say, yesterday, Lana and I were talking about how it's like it almost feels like there's no kind of... like it almost feels like things could just kind of flow out, you know, like I'm not responsible for it.

315
00:33:00,530 --> 00:33:03,932
[SPEAKER_00]: It just was in my mind.

316
00:33:04,392 --> 00:33:17,060
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you need to see, like, that is a sign that there's development to be had, because you would see, okay, if there is that sense of connection, means

317
00:33:17,740 --> 00:33:20,302
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not taking responsibility by default for what...

318
00:33:20,642 --> 00:33:24,406
[SPEAKER_00]: There has to be some sort of buffer, like, before you say things.

319
00:33:25,807 --> 00:33:28,149
[SPEAKER_00]: And that would be cultivated on account of the restraint.

320
00:33:28,169 --> 00:33:37,076
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if you have been restrained beforehand, your mind inclines towards, as the Buddha would say, you incline the mind in different directions.

321
00:33:37,096 --> 00:33:37,437
[SPEAKER_00]: So then,

322
00:33:38,317 --> 00:33:44,606
[SPEAKER_00]: Even way before you actually start falling into these traps, you would see yourself going that direction.

323
00:33:45,107 --> 00:33:53,639
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it only ends up in that case where it feels like, whether it be by speech, body, body speech or mind, it only gets to this point where...

324
00:33:54,660 --> 00:34:04,642
[SPEAKER_00]: It feels like the unwholesome is almost like happening by itself, because beforehand, you were overlooking the little step-by-step progression towards that full-on...

325
00:34:05,122 --> 00:34:08,803
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like a snowball, and then you don't stop it at the right time.

326
00:34:09,343 --> 00:34:10,163
[SPEAKER_00]: It just keeps going.

327
00:34:10,783 --> 00:34:18,865
[SPEAKER_00]: So, but that's connected to the other thing you were saying now, that the gossip and stuff, you wouldn't be able to tell

328
00:34:20,786 --> 00:34:28,391
[SPEAKER_00]: If you had to determine what is wholesome and unwholesome by having some sort of list to analyze each topic...

329
00:34:28,671 --> 00:34:38,498
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, so, this is about this and this, and it's 3 p.m. right now, and he... That's impossible to actually keep up.

330
00:34:38,558 --> 00:34:40,439
[SPEAKER_00]: The practice needs to be to the point where

331
00:34:42,280 --> 00:34:43,381
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't even need to reflect.

332
00:34:43,421 --> 00:34:45,583
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you already know before you even say it.

333
00:34:45,983 --> 00:34:47,705
[SPEAKER_00]: It's beyond doubt.

334
00:34:47,885 --> 00:34:48,966
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what the dhamma should be.

335
00:34:49,006 --> 00:34:58,193
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's only when you take it on the level of that pressure, as you mentioned, that you would start to see it doesn't matter even...

336
00:34:59,094 --> 00:35:03,217
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't even need to worry about the content of what I'm saying.

337
00:35:03,637 --> 00:35:07,160
[SPEAKER_00]: It won't be gossip if I'm not talking out of pressure.

338
00:35:07,340 --> 00:35:09,242
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, any useless topics,

339
00:35:09,982 --> 00:35:21,199
[SPEAKER_00]: You're like, by default, it won't even occur to you to talk about this, because you're not... that's that sort of stuff that truly serves no purpose, cannot come out unless you are feeling into pressure.

340
00:35:21,279 --> 00:35:21,579
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

341
00:35:21,900 --> 00:35:25,565
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you take that that as the standard, and then you ask yourself, well,

342
00:35:26,708 --> 00:35:33,750
[SPEAKER_00]: If I don't say this right now, is my mind going to start complaining about me not saying?

343
00:35:33,990 --> 00:35:35,951
[SPEAKER_00]: That pressure starts to build up.

344
00:35:36,551 --> 00:35:44,674
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you can see, oh, if I speak, I'm actually kind of furthering the agitation of this wild animal.

345
00:35:44,854 --> 00:35:45,854
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you just keep going.

346
00:35:46,720 --> 00:35:50,102
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you are surprised when you try to stop it and you can't.

347
00:35:50,183 --> 00:35:53,465
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's because you've been letting it run wild.

348
00:35:53,525 --> 00:35:57,487
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's where you... that's where the training is.

349
00:35:57,548 --> 00:36:00,189
[SPEAKER_00]: And seeing that... when am I speaking out of pressure?

350
00:36:00,590 --> 00:36:05,033
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you start to see the mouth as a kind of gateway that

351
00:36:06,074 --> 00:36:11,902
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter how much... like... like a dam that's holding water.

352
00:36:12,383 --> 00:36:15,668
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter how big the pressure gets to let the water out.

353
00:36:16,208 --> 00:36:19,193
[SPEAKER_00]: You are the one who has to actually open the dam and let it flow.

354
00:36:19,213 --> 00:36:19,413
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

355
00:36:19,593 --> 00:36:20,675
[SPEAKER_00]: It can't happen by itself.

356
00:36:21,116 --> 00:36:21,396
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

357
00:36:22,297 --> 00:36:24,819
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what you were saying, like it's three different domains.

358
00:36:25,059 --> 00:36:29,021
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter what you're thinking about and how much you want to say it.

359
00:36:29,682 --> 00:36:34,345
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't, if you succeed in not saying it, that's, that's all that matters.

360
00:36:35,005 --> 00:36:44,071
[SPEAKER_01]: And so... Yeah, but we should also now add that it, that we cannot fully go to the other side of the extreme and start determining to not say anything at all.

361
00:36:44,371 --> 00:36:44,731
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

362
00:36:44,791 --> 00:36:48,173
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'm saying, like, I don't mean not talking.

363
00:36:48,334 --> 00:36:58,480
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, when you see that there is the pressure, and then you refrain from that in that, like, with that background recognition, that's already a form of sati.

364
00:36:58,520 --> 00:37:01,061
[SPEAKER_00]: Actually, it's good to bring that up, because the

365
00:37:02,042 --> 00:37:18,641
[SPEAKER_00]: There's even the Buddha forbade this vow of silence, which is, ironically, the principle with which, like, I think, most meditation retreats operate, that you can't... you can't speak at all, or... or, like, just one or two words per day or something, because...

366
00:37:20,803 --> 00:37:36,176
[SPEAKER_00]: The reason why that's wrong is because you're not even training yourself so that when you get out of there, you actually see what are the unwholesome intentions behind my speech that were making me stressed, agitated and lustful.

367
00:37:36,696 --> 00:37:40,777
[SPEAKER_00]: When I wasn't in this retreat, you didn't learn anything about this.

368
00:37:41,257 --> 00:37:46,118
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but people will be able, like, when they hear this, I'm pretty sure they will be able to relate that.

369
00:37:46,138 --> 00:37:49,039
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, it's, in a sense, it's much easier to just determine.

370
00:37:49,059 --> 00:37:50,839
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not going to say anything for 10 days.

371
00:37:51,540 --> 00:38:00,602
[SPEAKER_01]: Rather than leave that 10% window, where it's still okay to say something, because that now forces you to question, is it really necessary for me to say this?

372
00:38:00,862 --> 00:38:02,162
[SPEAKER_00]: That's actually much more unpleasant.

373
00:38:02,202 --> 00:38:04,943
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, and then that will force you to learn

374
00:38:05,743 --> 00:38:07,546
[SPEAKER_00]: What is wholesome and what is unwholesome.

375
00:38:07,746 --> 00:38:12,393
[SPEAKER_00]: And you won't be trying to wriggle out of the problem so easily.

376
00:38:12,694 --> 00:38:13,935
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you can't.

377
00:38:14,156 --> 00:38:17,000
[SPEAKER_00]: It's factually, at some point, you will have to speak.

378
00:38:17,881 --> 00:38:19,484
[SPEAKER_00]: And that goes with everything.

379
00:38:20,665 --> 00:38:37,972
[SPEAKER_00]: In general, the practices, the sorts of practices that people take on for running away from things and burying their head in the sand for a while, that's the kind of peace it gives you, but you haven't discerned why, when your head was out of the sand, why things were a problem.

380
00:38:38,293 --> 00:38:38,473
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

381
00:38:38,593 --> 00:38:47,897
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're not really, well, you're not fixing the problem that the Buddha talked about, like that ignorance, not knowing suffering, not knowing the escape from suffering,

382
00:38:48,813 --> 00:38:51,816
[SPEAKER_00]: On account of which, all of these wrong things come to be.

383
00:38:51,916 --> 00:38:54,198
[SPEAKER_00]: Wrong speech, wrong action, wrong livelihood.

384
00:38:54,718 --> 00:39:12,153
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that is... that can only be done by, as we've been saying so many times, the intention behind your action, being clear about it, and seeing that as a training.

385
00:39:12,593 --> 00:39:14,114
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, day and night, you

386
00:39:14,995 --> 00:39:24,783
[SPEAKER_00]: You are aware of whether your actions by, as you said in the Rahula Sutta, you would be reflecting, before I do it, is this really a pressure?

387
00:39:25,123 --> 00:39:26,844
[SPEAKER_00]: While I'm doing it, is this really a pressure?

388
00:39:27,345 --> 00:39:29,006
[SPEAKER_00]: After I did it, is it really a pressure?

389
00:39:29,046 --> 00:39:31,028
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, that is actually teaching you.

390
00:39:31,428 --> 00:39:33,269
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, because you learn from your mistakes.

391
00:39:34,330 --> 00:39:44,117
[SPEAKER_00]: By not taking this blind vow of silence, and then you look back and you reflect, oh wait, on that occasion, I have a feeling that that was that was not wholesome, and then you see why.

392
00:39:44,837 --> 00:39:54,924
[SPEAKER_00]: Was it because of some external situation, or was it because at that moment I had this mental state of greed, aversion, delusion?

393
00:39:54,944 --> 00:39:56,905
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so that's what you recollect, the mental state.

394
00:39:56,985 --> 00:39:57,446
[SPEAKER_00]: And then,

395
00:39:58,653 --> 00:40:01,575
[SPEAKER_00]: You will actually remember that mental state, because you know in the past.

396
00:40:01,955 --> 00:40:05,637
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you can look back and see what are the motivations.

397
00:40:05,697 --> 00:40:13,701
[SPEAKER_00]: Even though it was a past event, you can see yourself, like, why you did something on that level that matters, that level of intention.

398
00:40:13,741 --> 00:40:17,563
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, when it comes up again in the future, you have a reference point.

399
00:40:17,583 --> 00:40:20,465
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, wait, this is the same thing that I did the other day.

400
00:40:20,885 --> 00:40:28,309
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you start seeing it, again, in the mental internal level, where the

401
00:40:29,152 --> 00:40:34,756
[SPEAKER_00]: The level of intention, on account of which, actual wrong speech can come to be.

402
00:40:34,997 --> 00:40:42,843
[SPEAKER_04]: And that, like, oh, I did this, this is the same kind of situation as the other day, that doesn't even mean, like, it's the same from the outside.

403
00:40:42,883 --> 00:40:44,924
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, it could be completely different.

404
00:40:45,064 --> 00:40:45,685
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, yeah.

405
00:40:46,288 --> 00:40:48,330
[SPEAKER_04]: The image is just totally different.

406
00:40:48,490 --> 00:40:49,271
[SPEAKER_04]: One, you're alone.

407
00:40:50,132 --> 00:40:51,413
[SPEAKER_04]: One, you're surrounded by people.

408
00:40:52,874 --> 00:40:56,898
[SPEAKER_04]: The actual content of what's happening is not... Yeah, yeah, yeah.

409
00:40:56,938 --> 00:40:59,321
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it could be absolutely anything.

410
00:41:00,642 --> 00:41:01,783
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the...

411
00:41:02,444 --> 00:41:06,488
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, the things you say, for example, I was going to bring that up.

412
00:41:07,429 --> 00:41:27,165
[SPEAKER_00]: The example of Mahākassapa, when he would be, you know, he called Ānanda, he called him a boy, and that, I mean, he's the Buddha's attendant, and that was, that sort of language would have been, or that sort of attitude would have probably been considered, like, how can an arahant say something like that to someone?

413
00:41:27,626 --> 00:41:32,210
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, on the other occasion, like, when they were on the council,

414
00:41:32,870 --> 00:41:37,692
[SPEAKER_00]: He forced Ānanda to confess a bunch of offenses, to see them as wrongdoings.

415
00:41:38,292 --> 00:41:49,797
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, in general, he was just this kind of... like, he refused to... to shrug the monks, because he said, these days, monks are full of evil wishes, and they don't... they don't actually care about the dhamma.

416
00:41:50,337 --> 00:42:01,283
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you know, he... that could be... by people who think the the the speech is about, as you said, the content of what you say, that could be considered as a as a form of wrong speech.

417
00:42:01,303 --> 00:42:17,773
[SPEAKER_00]: But because there would have not been any sort of predilection for greed, aversion, delusion behind him saying that, which only he could know, then... or another person, like, who also has the right view, then

418
00:42:19,962 --> 00:42:21,003
[SPEAKER_00]: There wasn't any problem with it.

419
00:42:21,023 --> 00:42:27,505
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, if you don't take responsibility, and you think, oh, well, look, that monk speaks like that, so I can also do it.

420
00:42:28,046 --> 00:42:28,366
[SPEAKER_00]: But no.

421
00:42:28,526 --> 00:42:36,689
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, you would think, you would still be putting it in the content, and you wouldn't be taking responsibility for seeing this mind.

422
00:42:37,449 --> 00:42:38,770
[SPEAKER_00]: What state is it in right now?

423
00:42:39,550 --> 00:42:41,271
[SPEAKER_00]: And, yeah, different people

424
00:42:42,609 --> 00:42:51,159
[SPEAKER_02]: After you understand the way of suffering and stuff, you would have different... still different tendencies and ways of talking or whatever.

425
00:42:51,760 --> 00:43:02,953
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, some people might be more naturally, you know, meek and gently spoken, and other people might be a little bit

426
00:43:03,453 --> 00:43:04,975
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, more abrasive.

427
00:43:06,036 --> 00:43:15,606
[SPEAKER_00]: And that doesn't really mean anything fundamentally on the internal level, as long as

428
00:43:16,903 --> 00:43:18,843
[SPEAKER_00]: There are obvious things that you cannot do.

429
00:43:18,863 --> 00:43:20,744
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you can't go insulting people because...

430
00:43:21,264 --> 00:43:29,326
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, that is what is defined literally as wrong speech in the in the formula that I just mentioned.

431
00:43:29,346 --> 00:43:34,367
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, that is the obvious stuff that if you do it, you're automatically falling to wrong speech.

432
00:43:35,107 --> 00:43:40,428
[SPEAKER_00]: Also, eh... another thing I thought was important to bring up, eh...

433
00:43:41,168 --> 00:43:47,993
[SPEAKER_00]: It's also mentioned in the suttas many times that you shouldn't exalt yourself and put others down.

434
00:43:48,173 --> 00:43:55,398
[SPEAKER_00]: And I think that is that's also included in the in the context of right speech sometimes.

435
00:43:55,658 --> 00:43:58,219
[SPEAKER_02]: Because...

436
00:43:58,239 --> 00:44:00,641
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the thing with that is usually the assumption is...

437
00:44:01,522 --> 00:44:03,343
[SPEAKER_02]: So, criticism is bad, essentially.

438
00:44:05,324 --> 00:44:08,847
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you should be focusing on bringing people up,

439
00:44:09,867 --> 00:44:12,889
[SPEAKER_00]: Instead of putting them down.

440
00:44:12,969 --> 00:44:19,512
[SPEAKER_00]: But the thing is, like, it doesn't say... it says you refrain from exalting yourself and putting others down.

441
00:44:19,993 --> 00:44:24,295
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't say you ensure that you put yourself down and exalt others.

442
00:44:25,658 --> 00:44:29,801
[SPEAKER_01]: No, because that would be a sort of self-denigration, which is also unwholesome.

443
00:44:29,901 --> 00:44:38,728
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, or not even full-on self-denigration, like trying to almost artificially kind of focus on the good qualities.

444
00:44:38,748 --> 00:44:44,532
[SPEAKER_00]: Like someone's promoting wrong view, but you see, oh, you try to find like the positive aspect of it.

445
00:44:44,772 --> 00:44:44,992
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

446
00:44:45,072 --> 00:44:48,255
[SPEAKER_00]: So as to, you know, not put yourself up and

447
00:44:48,535 --> 00:44:51,037
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, sometimes with somebody's behavior you can do that.

448
00:44:51,117 --> 00:44:54,639
[SPEAKER_01]: If you are about to do something unwholesome, you can do that.

449
00:44:54,719 --> 00:45:01,003
[SPEAKER_01]: But there are indeed other discourses where the Buddha even says how should one monk admonish another.

450
00:45:01,023 --> 00:45:01,263
[SPEAKER_01]: Right?

451
00:45:01,703 --> 00:45:09,488
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, no, but... And then it should be like beneficial, the timing should be right, with a mind of friendliness, and so on.

452
00:45:09,748 --> 00:45:16,973
[SPEAKER_01]: So the whole idea that to not exalt oneself does not mean that one cannot, let's say,

453
00:45:22,397 --> 00:45:26,239
[SPEAKER_00]: No, but I wasn't referring to, like, a person you're actually talking to.

454
00:45:26,480 --> 00:45:27,100
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, I see.

455
00:45:27,160 --> 00:45:35,206
[SPEAKER_02]: I was referring to in the sense of views, like, when the Buddha, for example, said, you shouldn't be... he actually defined it in this

456
00:45:36,767 --> 00:45:47,580
[SPEAKER_00]: Sutta in the Majjhima Nikaya, he said, how is it that a person falls into this denigration and self-exaltation, and how is it that they only speak dhamma?

457
00:45:48,681 --> 00:45:57,090
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that is, like, people might think, again, that it's just by criticizing other views,

458
00:45:57,951 --> 00:46:03,956
[SPEAKER_00]: You are somehow being considered arrogant that you need to kind of allow space for other people to be right as well.

459
00:46:03,976 --> 00:46:11,902
[SPEAKER_00]: But the thing is... the thing is... as the Buddha said, how do you... how do you avoid that?

460
00:46:11,922 --> 00:46:22,470
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't avoid it by not point... by not pointing out that there is right and wrong, which is again kind of an easy way out of the situation and doesn't fix it at all.

461
00:46:22,490 --> 00:46:23,371
[SPEAKER_00]: It's... it's by

462
00:46:24,812 --> 00:46:34,775
[SPEAKER_00]: Instead of saying, talking in terms of me, like, I am wrong, you are wrong, I've refuted your argument, you're, you're... No, I see what you mean, yes.

463
00:46:35,616 --> 00:46:39,677
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you, you instead say, whoever is practicing like this... Yeah.

464
00:46:40,658 --> 00:46:42,219
[SPEAKER_00]: They are attached to being.

465
00:46:42,439 --> 00:46:45,621
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the literal way the Buddha explained it.

466
00:46:45,942 --> 00:46:48,563
[SPEAKER_00]: Whoever has this sort of practice, they're attached to being.

467
00:46:48,804 --> 00:46:51,365
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't see the way out of craving.

468
00:46:52,306 --> 00:46:53,086
[SPEAKER_00]: They have wrong view.

469
00:46:53,487 --> 00:46:56,208
[SPEAKER_00]: Whoever practices this way, they have right view.

470
00:46:56,289 --> 00:46:57,309
[SPEAKER_00]: They see the way out of craving.

471
00:46:57,369 --> 00:46:57,990
[SPEAKER_00]: Instead of saying,

472
00:46:58,810 --> 00:47:04,819
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, these people are arahants, and these people are total buddhismists.

473
00:47:05,280 --> 00:47:09,285
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't focus on the person, specifically.

474
00:47:09,325 --> 00:47:10,206
[SPEAKER_00]: You focus on the views.

475
00:47:10,347 --> 00:47:12,690
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you criticize, and that's what the Buddha was doing.

476
00:47:12,730 --> 00:47:15,173
[SPEAKER_00]: He was criticizing the views that people had.

477
00:47:15,754 --> 00:47:34,382
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like personally attacking people, and that's really... personally attacking or personally exalting himself, like to make himself feel, you know, make himself appear in that kind of personal sense, like there's some sort of contest between us and I'm trying to come out on top.

478
00:47:35,102 --> 00:47:39,765
[SPEAKER_00]: That's really when you end up exalting yourself and denigrating others.

479
00:47:40,225 --> 00:47:41,025
[SPEAKER_00]: How would you tell?

480
00:47:41,045 --> 00:47:44,767
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, a very practical sense, a practical test that you can do,

481
00:47:45,787 --> 00:47:55,373
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, if you are criticizing somebody's views, but then, do you feel like you actually now have a personal problem with that person as well?

482
00:47:55,814 --> 00:48:08,882
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if you had to talk to meet that person whose views you're criticizing, would you have some sort of internal animosity or would you be able to treat them totally like a like a normal person on a kind of

483
00:48:10,623 --> 00:48:18,089
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, the usual polite way that you would treat somebody else, or would you be like, that's that idiot that believes this.

484
00:48:18,369 --> 00:48:34,041
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's how you would know if your speech is really rooted in... Yeah, and this goes back to the question of... to the discussion of patience that we had, like khanti, like the highest discipline or the highest austerity, but it's also paired with gentleness, often.

485
00:48:34,201 --> 00:48:35,602
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, that's the aim of ascetics.

486
00:48:35,962 --> 00:48:39,325
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to endure, still, somebody else's wrong view,

487
00:48:39,965 --> 00:48:43,028
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, are you going to challenge somebody?

488
00:48:43,328 --> 00:48:51,056
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if those views are actually wrong, are you going to challenge that because it's more pleasing for you to not have to suffer somebody else's wrong view?

489
00:48:51,557 --> 00:48:54,399
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, is the dhamma just about stating what is the right view?

490
00:48:55,280 --> 00:49:00,902
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you averse to the displeasure of seeing that someone has a wrong view and you're trying to call it out?

491
00:49:01,423 --> 00:49:09,506
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when you will automatically end up debating on that personal level and the sutta describes actually that sort of a back and forth thing.

492
00:49:09,786 --> 00:49:11,547
[SPEAKER_00]: It says like, eh...

493
00:49:11,867 --> 00:49:21,731
[SPEAKER_00]: I am right, you are wrong, what you said before, what you said after, you said before, what you said before, you said after, I totally contradicted everything you say, go back, you're, you know,

494
00:49:21,991 --> 00:49:23,292
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, try to rescue your t-shirt.

495
00:49:23,332 --> 00:49:25,814
[SPEAKER_00]: Try to rescue your t-shirt if you can.

496
00:49:26,034 --> 00:49:29,816
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's a very... you see, that's not something that can happen by accident.

497
00:49:29,836 --> 00:49:32,818
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, just because you said, well, this sort of practice is wrong.

498
00:49:33,258 --> 00:49:39,442
[SPEAKER_00]: That's like a full-on you attacking somebody because you have a... you have... you are upset internally.

499
00:49:39,803 --> 00:49:40,263
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

500
00:49:40,463 --> 00:49:48,268
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's a very different thing because the Buddha spent all the time almost, like, half of the stuff he was saying was pointing at wrong view.

501
00:49:48,348 --> 00:49:49,109
[SPEAKER_00]: The other half was

502
00:49:49,889 --> 00:49:52,191
[SPEAKER_00]: Once that's done, then you know what's right.

503
00:49:52,291 --> 00:50:00,458
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can't just decide that... you can't point out what's wrong because...

504
00:50:00,478 --> 00:50:06,163
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, he himself said, not pointing out non-dhamma as non-dhamma.

505
00:50:07,243 --> 00:50:12,188
